{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And the days of… drew near.</b> I have already written nearby whence he knew, and Ramban wrote that he knew from the sensation of his strength failing. But his words, may his memory be for blessing, do not seem [correct], for how many days might a person live after he begins to weaken?" "<b>Further,</b> one may say that he sensed it from the things that happen to a person before his death, in the manner of what they said, may their memory be for blessing, in the Zohar (part 1, 217b): that thirty days before a person's death the tzelem is removed from him, and an incident is brought there that Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai saw Rabbi Isaac whose tzelem had departed from him. And although this knowledge is withheld from the children of men, the righteous sense and know every spiritual thing. The matter will be further explained in examining again the verse's saying <b>and the days of… drew near</b> — but is it applicable to days to speak in the language of drawing near?" "<b>However,</b> the intent of the verse is according to what the holy man of Elohim wrote — he is the Rav, Rabbi Isaac Luria, may the memory of the righteous and holy one be for blessing — and you will find it when you investigate it in the book Kehillat Yaakov, and this is his language: 'Now, you know from the division of souls into several sparks, and in each and every incarnation some of them come, and according to the number of sparks of the tzelem, so is the number of days of his life. And the days on which he performs mitzvot, one spark of that tzelem corresponding to that day is repaired; and the day on which he does not perform a mitzvah, that spark of the tzelem corresponding to that day remains blemished; and so on in this manner,' etc. — thus far." "And this introduction greatly enlightened the eyes of understanding in several details of investigation. First, it enlightened our eyes in the sod of sleep, which is to elevate the spark corresponding to that day; and this is the sod of their saying that sleep is one sixtieth of death (Berakhot 57b). And the emergence of the spark is not absolutely forced out of the soul, but rather it separates from everything while still attached and entangled in the nefesh. And this is from the lovingkindness of El, that every spark that merited on its day — behold, it is far from destruction even if a person sins afterward. And a spark that ascends blemished — behold, it can be repaired through teshuvah; the spark returns and is repaired. This too is from the lovingkindnesses of the One who acts with lovingkindness, who is our Elohim, may His name be exalted." "It also illuminated before our eyes the response to the outcry among the children of men, among the children of man: 'What is this that Elohim has done to us? How have our generations changed from the earlier generations, who were sated with life nine hundred years and thirty for one man [Adam], and likewise his sons and his sons' sons eight hundred and seven hundred — while we, the most extraordinary among the living, do not reach a hundred years?' And according to this introduction, the mind of every person finds rest. And the parable for this: a king who distributed precious stones coming from the quarry to craftsmen to refine them to the utmost beauty and preparation, and to make for them settings of gold in the manner of royal treasures. And he decreed that the diligent man who strives to perform the refinement with all effort and with all refinement and beauty as the king commanded — the precious stones would be his portion and inheritance, to delight and take pleasure in them. And he gave the stones in a very great number to each and every man: to one he gave three hundred thousand and to another three hundred and fifty thousand, whether fewer or more. And he fixed for them a time that would suffice for the work — one day for each and every stone — and when the days were complete, the king would send to take the precious stones. And it came to pass when the days of the work were completed, the king inspected his craftsmen who worked in the craft of the stones, and behold, they were gathered together and presented before the king their deposit. And not only did they not perform upon them the work of refinement and beauty, but they further ruined them and soiled them and returned them in their ruined state. And the king grew angry over the matter and set fire to all those servants, and he established their sons in their stead and showed them the breach that their fathers had breached in this manner. And he charged them and girded them not to do as their fathers had done. And he ruled not to multiply for them the work, and lightened the burden upon them, and gave them thirty thousand to forty thousand. And this was the choice of kindness toward his servants — perhaps they would be able to prepare; and also on account of the closeness of the king's inspection, they might give heed." "<b>Behold</b> — these are the matters spoken of the King of Israel, the Elohim of the universe, blessed be He, who apportioned precious stones; these are the souls called precious stones. This is the sod of what is said (Genesis 49:24): ״מִשָּׁם רוֹעֶה אֶבֶן יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — for through the instrumentality of the vessels and the preparation that He prepared in our hands, which is the Torah and His commandments, may He be blessed, to illuminate them and to perform them in the proper rectification, along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for blessing (Zohar vol. 1, 2b): that through the strengthening of a person in doing good and distancing from evil according to the Torah, he produces for his soul a supreme elevation, and illuminates its light from the light of Torah, and prepares for it a throne — and this is the aspect of the settings that we wrote in the parable — and also acquires for it a crown of kingship." "And to the first ones HaShem began to multiply for them the task, and He gave them great souls in each of which there are many parts, and He limited for each part its task to one day. And this is the sod of (Exodus 16:4): ״דְּבַר יוֹם בְּיוֹמוֹ״, for the branch is called a day, and He gave them many days corresponding to the parts that are in their souls. To Adam He gave more than three hundred thousand parts, which is the number of his days, 970, and by this measure they are either less or more for those generations. And when they corrupted and HaShem broke forth against them a breach and established their sons in their stead — Noah and his sons — from there onward HaShem lightened giving that great burden, so that the recall would be near and people would tremble from the time of recall. And when He saw that they could not endure, He would diminish further and lighten; and this day, because of our sins, the years of our life in them are seventy years, which is approximately twenty-five thousand days, and many of the children of our people have not stood up even in a light service such as this." "<b>Furthermore</b>, through this we have found relief in what we see: for when a person ages his understanding diminishes, and even a wise man cannot go out and come in to the battle of Torah and his sensations diminish. Although they said, may their memory be for blessing (Kinnim 3:6): elders of Torah — their mind settles upon them — they were precise to say ״settles״ to exclude the mind of the elders of the ignorant, which becomes deranged; and in any case the power of the intellect and understanding is nullified. And according to this introduction HaShem enlightened the eye in this matter: for since all the parts of the soul have departed, there is no strength in the remainder to perform according to the measure of the whole; also the body cannot sustain itself and part of it will weigh upon part, because of the diminution of what nourishes and feeds, which is the soul, and he reverts to the mind of a child." "<b>Furthermore</b>, through this introduction we have found relief in the intent of the statement of our Sages, may their memory be for blessing (Genesis Rabbah 62:2), who said: ״What is the difference between the death of elders and the death of youths? The death of elders, etc. is good for the lamp and good for the wick,״ etc. — thus far. They likened the death of the elder to a lamp: just as a lamp steadily diminishes, so too the elder — the parts of the soul have already departed; and the final part that remains in him, upon its departure, will be likened for him to a sleeping person who does not feel the departure of the part from him. And understand." "<b>Furthermore</b>, through this we have found the explanation of what the verse says (Psalms 104:29): ״תֹּסֵף רוּחָם יִגְוָעוּן״, for the intent of the verse is not well known, nor did it say ״תֹּסֵף בְּרוּחָם״. And according to the introduction the light of the verse will shine like the light of the sun: that the intent is to say that when a person's end arrives, the recall of his soul, all the parts of the soul that wandered and departed night after night are added back into him — and this is what it says: ״תֹּסֵף רוּחָם״ — meaning their spirit itself. And we have already said that although it departs, it is not uprooted absolutely; rather, it is like the departure of the soul at night at the time of sleep: it casts its light in the body below while it is above. And here is your sign: when a person wakes the sleeper, the part that awakens will feel, and he will also feel the part that has ascended — and understand." "<b>Furthermore,</b> we have broadened our understanding that those portions of the soul are called ימים ('days'). And thereby the meaning of the verse that we have arrived at is illuminated — where it says <b>וַיִּקְרְבוּ יְמֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לָמוּת</b>, meaning, the portions of the soul that had ascended from him day by day had now drawn near, as the verse says (Psalms 104:29): ״תֹּסֵף רוּחָם״ — and this is what it means by ״לָמוּת״. And our father Jacob sensed the great increase, for the days of reckoning had come: ״וַיִּקְרָא לִבְנוֹ לְיוֹסֵף״ etc." "<b>וַיִּקְרָא לִבְנוֹ לְיוֹסֵף וְגוֹ׳</b>. As for the reason he called only to Joseph, there is a simple reason in the matter — because he had the ability. Rather, one needs to know the reason for his saying <b>לִבְנוֹ</b> ('to his son') and not being satisfied to say לְיוֹסֵף ('to Joseph'), for we have no other Joseph to be mistaken for. Also, he added the word לְ before יוֹסֵף and was not satisfied to say לִבְנוֹ יוֹסֵף ('to his son Joseph')." "It appears that the verse comes to give a reason for the calling: how could a commoner summon a king? For have we not found that the honor of royalty takes precedence over the honor of one's master, as they deliberated in tractate Kiddushin (33a) in a question regarding 'his son who is his master,' and the Gemara there said, when it sought to clarify the statement of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi who said: 'I am not worthy to stand before my son except because of the honor of the house of the Nasi.' And I have already elaborated on this sugya, by the good hand of HaShem upon me, in my second work (Rishon le-Tziyon) that I composed on Yoreh De'ah, siman 240, se'if 3 — there one may review my words. And in any event, from the words of the Talmud it is explicitly proven that the obligation to honor the house of the Nasi is great upon his father, and even if his son himself were not the Nasi but the son-in-law of the house of the Nasi. If so, how could Jacob do such a thing — to send to summon the king to come to him? Therefore the verse gives two reasons for the matter: the first, his being his son, and the second, his being Joseph. With either one alone it would not suffice to summon him — if he were not his son, even if he were righteous and beloved and a lover of him, Jacob would have no power to do such a thing because of the honor of the king; and if he were his son but not righteous, likewise he would not call him. Rather, it was because he was his son, which is what it means by <b>לִבְנוֹ</b>, and also because he was Joseph, known for his righteousness, known for the extraordinary love of the father for him and he for his father — for this reason he sent to summon him, for he knew that he would waive his honor. And although they said, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 32a), 'a king who waives his honor — his honor is not waived,' that applies specifically to a king of Israel who was anointed and ruled over Israel; but a king such as this, in this situation, may waive his honor to do his father's will, even in a circumstance where his father cannot come to him." "<b>אִם נָא מָצָאתִי חֵן.</b> The word <b>נָא</b> may be interpreted both as a term of request and as a term meaning 'now': as a term of request — to request an instance of finding favor that is not on account of what preceded it, namely the obligation incumbent upon him. And as a term meaning 'now,' the intention is to hint to him that Jacob already knew that he had found favor in Joseph's eyes, and here is your sign (Genesis 47:12): ״וַיְכַלְכֵּל יוֹסֵף אֶת אָבִיו״ etc. And from now on, if Jacob had said to him 'אִם מָצָאתִי חֵן' ('if I have found favor'), this would indicate that this matter — that he had found favor in his eyes — was not acknowledged by him. Therefore he said 'now,' meaning that the finding of favor he requests of him is for what is 'now.'" "<b>Or</b> it may intend that since his sun had reached its setting, this would bring an end to the need for the customary kindness until now from Joseph toward him. Therefore he said to him: if now you still wish to continue in the conduct of showing favor, שִׂים etc." "<b>Further,</b> by way of remez he intended to hint to him that all the kindness he had done with him until now involved two sides: the first, that he acted because Jacob found favor in his eyes not for any ulterior purpose, and this would be on the side of genuine love, arising from the fact that he found favor. The second, that he acted out of obligation, for he is obligated to sustain his father as is the way of all the world, and it would be a disgrace to him to cast his father and brothers upon others and to see them lacking bread and food. Therefore he said to him that the matter which was in doubt as to what his intention had been, the doubt will be clarified by what he now asks of him. If now he does for him his desire and will, it becomes known retroactively that the good things he did for him were because he found favor in his eyes with love and heartfelt desire — for the thing he asks of him this time is not something he would need to do unless his love is genuine and whole and he desires to do him good, and this is a revelation about the past, that ״מָצָאתִי חֵן בְּעֵינֶיךָ״. And if not, it becomes clear that the reason for the past kindness is the second reason we have written, and in this one may press the force of his saying <b>מָצָאתִי</b> in the past tense." "<b>שִׂים נָא.</b> This may be explained as an expression of time and as an expression of request. As an expression of request — besides the fact that he already used the language of request in connection with the act, he returned to speak a request a second time, for not only would he agree to the kindness and tell him to do so, he further sought to placate him to swear to him concerning the kindness to fulfill it. And it may be explained as an expression of 'now,' that he intended to forestall from him the answer of 'still today' — that he would do this thing that day — for it was not the time of death, as you find that he said after this ״וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה״ etc. From this you learn that it was not then at the point of departure. Therefore he said to him ״שִׂים עַתָּה״ — without the argument of waiting until the time comes when the word of the supreme King arrives. Also, Jacob was concerned about the giving of prior notice concerning the matter, and he would preempt with an oath to nullify this oath, and would swear to him an oath that lacks full intent. And all the more so according to their words, of blessed memory, who said (Tanhuma) that Jacob said to him that if he would not swear to him, behold he would die of anguish, and it is alluded to in his saying ״וְשָׁכַבְתִּי עִם״ etc. If so, Joseph — out of the necessity of the situation — would swear after preempting with a prior oath to nullify this oath. Therefore he said to him <b>שִׂים נָא</b>, meaning now, immediately and at once, and he deprived him of the time in which he might give notice to nullify this oath." "<b>וְעָשִׂיתָ עִמָּדִי חֶסֶד וֶאֱמֶת.</b> He said <b>וְעָשִׂיתָ</b> with an added vav, because since the request to perform the oath preceded, he said ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״ with an added vav." "And his saying <b>חֶסֶד וֶאֱמֶת</b> — our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 96:5) that kindness done for the dead is called חֶסֶד שֶׁל אֱמֶת (kindness of truth). And it is difficult for me: do we not find that Jacob repaid him for this kindness, as it is said (Genesis 48:22) ״וַאֲנִי נָתַתִּי לְךָ שְׁכֶם אַחַד״ etc., and Rashi explained: you toil for my burial and I have given etc. Moreover, his saying ״נָתַתִּי״ in the past tense indicates that he informed him of the matter at the time of the act, when he accepted the toil upon himself; and even if this is not stated, Jacob at any rate had it in mind to reward him for his toil, so he should not have said ״וֶאֱמֶת״." "Or perhaps he intended that Joseph would do the thing in this manner. Or it may be that in proportion to the act, the recompense he would be given — Shechem one — would not be recognized. Or it may be because he asked of him two things: the first, that he not bury him in Egypt and that he strive to bring him forth from a city of idolatry; and the second, that he himself bring him up and bury him in the land of Canaan. And he spoke of חֶסֶד שֶׁל אֱמֶת with regard to the aspect of the removal from the land of Egypt, which is what he said immediately adjacent to his saying <b>חֶסֶד וֶאֱמֶת אַל נָא תִּקְבְּרֵנִי בְּמִצְרָיִם.</b> Now, it would have appeared proper for him to mention the lying down first and afterward the burial, yet here he mentioned the burial before his saying ״וְשָׁכַבְתִּי״. This clearly shows that it was concerning this particular point that the oath was directed, and this matter has no recompense, neither now nor later. And what he gave him — Shechem one — is for the toil of bringing him up with him from the land of Egypt to the land of Canaan and burying him there." "The verse may further be explained along this line: <b>and you shall do</b> etc. — meaning, what you have already done with me shall be a true kindness, meaning the kindness of recompense, for what he gives him is double in consideration of what he is about to do, to toil in his burial — not for the past favor. And from this we may also scrutinize the word ״ועשית״ in the addition of the vav, and also in the past tense: the addition of the vav means that he will add to do good with him, in that he will consider the kindness he has already done to be a true one and will not accept a favor from him in return for it; and in this the favor coming from the one who administers the oath will be complete, and he swears upon the mind of his fellow because of the good that he did for him, which has no release (Ran on Nedarim 65a). And Jacob was shrewd lest Joseph release the oath after his death. And although we do not find that Jacob mentioned the favor of the one portion at that time, the verse revealed elsewhere that he had already mentioned it to him, as it is written ״נתתי לך״, and from the silence regarding the place of its mention, it was when he said to him ״השבעה לי״ — to strengthen the oath so that he not release [it], that he had given him one portion. And otherwise, on what would Jacob's mind rely in this oath, since he could release it afterward, as we elaborated on this detail in parashat Toldot (Genesis 26:26) regarding Isaac's oath to Abimelech. And what they said, of blessed memory (Sotah 36b), that Joseph said to Pharaoh: ״If I release my father's oath I will also release your oath״ — there is no contradiction from this, either because he responded to Pharaoh's words, or also because his oath to Pharaoh was for a good that he did for him, for there is no good as great as that — greatness, honor, and wealth." "<b>Please do not bury me</b> etc. Meaning, ״אל נא״ is an expression of time: that even for the moment they should not bury him on the assumption that he would later endeavor to bring him up, but rather he should immediately and forthwith bring him up — and this is what it says immediately afterward: <b>and I shall lie down</b> etc. <b>and you shall carry me</b> etc. And the reason for putting ״אל נא תקברני במצרים״ before his saying ״ושכבתי״ I explained in the verse ״ועשית עמדי״ etc., according to one of the approaches I settled upon in the verse. And according to the other approaches it will be explained in light of what has preceded us (Zohar Vayeshev 182a), that Jacob and Joseph were of the aspect of a single soul, and this is the sod of the full vav, which has two vavin and they do not separate; and therefore Jacob was concerned that after he was gathered he would not want Joseph to be separated from him even in death — and all the more so according to their words, of blessed memory (Ta'anit 5b), who said that Jacob our father did not die but is alive. Therefore he removed this matter from his mind and informed him that when he lies down — meaning, when he departs — he is perforce separated from him and joins his fathers, and therefore ״ונשאתני״ etc. ״וקברתני״ etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {6 "The number תת״קל (930, the numerical value) is rendered as 970; the text may intend 930, Adam's lifespan per Genesis 5:5.", 20 "ר״ן נדרים סה refers to the Ran's commentary on Nedarim 65a; סוטה לו: is Sotah 36b; תענית ה: is Ta'anit 5b."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:25.910004581Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}